            "Module 5 described the sample. The researcher, though, wants to talk about the population — not the 400 pregnant women measured in the clinic but all pregnant women in the district — and the moment she does, she must admit that a different 400 would have given a slightly different answer. <strong>Inferential statistics</strong> is the arithmetic of that admission: it uses what is known about how samples vary to say how far the truth about the population is likely to lie from the number the sample gave. Everything in this module rests on one idea. Imagine drawing not one sample of 400 women but a thousand samples of 400 from the same district and calculating the mean haemoglobin of each; the thousand means would not be identical, but they would cluster around the true population mean, with most close to it and a few further away, and — by the <strong>central limit theorem</strong> — they would form a normal distribution whatever the shape of the original variable, provided the samples were reasonably large. This distribution of sample means is narrower than the distribution of individual women, and its standard deviation is called the <strong>standard error</strong> of the mean: the standard deviation of the variable divided by the square root of the sample size, <strong>SE = <em>s</em> / √<em>n</em></strong>. If haemoglobin has a standard deviation of 1.2 g/dL and the sample is 400, the standard error is 1.2 ÷ 20 = 0.06 g/dL; with a sample of 100 it would be 0.12. The standard deviation describes how much <em>women</em> vary; the standard error describes how much <em>sample means</em> vary, and shrinks with the square root of the sample size — which is why four times the sample buys only half the uncertainty, and why precision is expensive. The same logic gives a standard error for a proportion, √[<em>p</em>(1 − <em>p</em>)/<em>n</em>], and for a difference between two means or two proportions.",
