            "The clinician's question, though, is the other way round: this child has tested positive — what is the chance she has malaria? That is the <strong>positive predictive value</strong>, true positives divided by all positives — 190/(190 + 40) = 83 per cent in this population — and the <strong>negative predictive value</strong> is true negatives divided by all negatives — 760/(760 + 10) = 99 per cent. The predictive values depend on the <strong>prevalence</strong> of the disease in the people tested, and this is the point the examiner presses: take the same test, with the same 95 per cent sensitivity and specificity, to a low-transmission area where only 20 of 1,000 febrile children have malaria, and the 980 without it will generate 49 false positives against 19 true positives — a positive predictive value of 28 per cent, so that most positive results are wrong, while the negative predictive value rises to nearly 100 per cent. The same arithmetic explains why a positive HIV screening test in a low-prevalence antenatal clinic must be confirmed with a second, different test; why screening the whole population for a rare disease produces mostly false alarms; and why a test's performance in a hospital study of sick patients overstates what it will do at a durbar. The <strong>likelihood ratio</strong> (sensitivity ÷ [1 − specificity] for a positive test) combines the two fixed properties into one number that can be applied to any prevalence, and, with the Wilson–Jungner criteria of lesson 5.2, is the last tool the nurse needs to judge whether a screening programme is worth running.",
